<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chalcidice</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Halkidiki</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chalcidice is a peninsula of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in Central Macedonia, ending in three prongs: Kassandra, Sithonia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a>. It took its name from the many colonies founded there by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcis">Chalcis</a> and other Greek cities from the 8th century BCE, which later formed the Chalcidian League around Olynthus until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> destroyed it in 348 BCE. Mount Athos is home to a self-governing monastic community.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chalcidice.jpg" alt="Chalcidice" width="320" height="285" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pitichinaccio · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2010_Dimi_Chalkidikis_numbered.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
